Right-wing populist triumph in Austria, combined with 29 per cent
According to the first preliminary results of Sunday's early parliamentary elections in Austria, a significant weakening of both parties and a significant strengthening of right-wing populist parties.
The Austrian People's Party (ÖVP) has suffered particularly strong losses, where the position of the current chairman, Wilhelm Molterer, is being seriously shaken.
On the contrary, the electoral leader of the Union for the Future of Austria (BZÖ), Carinthian Governor Jörg Haider is preparing a triumphant return to national politics.
According to the preliminary results, the strongest party in the country remains the social democracy (SPÖ), with 29.8 percent of the vote, but it loses 5.5 percent of the vote against the last 2006 elections.
The ÖVP, which loses nearly nine percent of the vote with 25.6 percent, was even worse.
These are the worst results of both of these major parties in Austrian post-war history, and especially among the people who triggered early elections, literally a disastrous result.
Even at the beginning of July, when the ÖVP withdrew from the Coalition, the people had a significant lead in their preferences before the SPÖ.
Voters, however, apparently punished them for letting the government fail.
Already in the first reactions to the results, there was speculation about the possible resignation of the chairman of the party and former Vice-Chancellor Wilhelm Molterer.
Observers believe that this could happen as early as Monday at an emergency meeting of the party's presidency.
Such a development would probably facilitate the path to an ever-most-probable exit from the election result, i.e. the resumption of a grand coalition of SPÖ and ÖVP.
However, due to the strengthening of the both right-wing populist parties - free (FPÖ) gained preliminary 18 percent and BZÖ eleven percent of the vote - the Social Democrats also voiced fears of a repeat in 1999, when the people's people agreed with populists (at that time they were still united, in 2005 they splittered from BZÖ) on a joint government, which triggered even sanctions from the European Union.
Haider, who has already stated that he is ready to cooperate with either party and foresees a return to Vienna into national politics.
The Green Party got into parliament, but it lost slightly and fell between the Austrian political parties from third to fifth place.
None of the other parties apparently crossed the four-percent threshold and did not make it to parliament.
Schwarzenberg: Relations with India can get cold
Failure to solve the problem with Czech scientists who have been accused of illegally collecting beetles in India, according to Czech Foreign Minister Karel Schwarzenberg, there would be a certain cooling of Czech-Indian relations.
The minister stated this in New York after today's meeting with his Indian counterpart Pranáb Mukherji.
"If it hadn't been resolved, then yes, then there would have been some cooling," he said after the meeting, which took place from the Czech initiative.
Mukhergi, after the hearing, said the executive could find it difficult to interfere in court matters.
However, he stated that in the case of Petr Švācha, who was acquitted and punished by a fine, the central government can negotiate with the local government in Darjiling, in whose jurisdiction the matter falls.
"His problem could be solved," the minister said.
It would be best for Emil Kučera sentenced to three years in prison, according to Mukherji, to appeal to a higher instance.
He can then apply for release on bail and wait for the verdict at liberty, he explained.
Schwarzenberg confirmed that his partner expressed himself the same way at the meeting.
"He recommends an appeal to a higher court, then it would be possible to pardon him," the head of Czech diplomacy said.
The only way the Czech side can join the matter is to provide "competent legal support" to the convicts, the Indian minister said.
At the same time, he said that such assistance has already been provided to them by the Indian side.
“We have the highest interest in maintaining the best relationships with India, which have a long tradition.
The Indians also recognize this," Schwarzenberg said.
At the same time, as ČTK told, he emphasized before his partner that the Czech Republic attaches great importance to the problem of Czech entomologists.
"We really consider it a profound mistake of imprisoning and indicting these Czech scientists," he said.
The minister avoided answering the question of whether he received a promise from the Indian side to deal with things.
"I've received quite a few promises," he said.
Czech judges fight over seabed
It proceeds to a wall-mounted electronic map showing Australia and adjacent marine areas.
However, he is not interested in the continent, but a small point lost in the blue of the ocean.
"The island belongs to Australia and, along with it, the adjoining seabed.
At the same time, the area is as big as the continent itself," says Italian lawyer Luigi Santosuosso, who belongs to a thirty-member team of workers at the U.N
Its boss, the director of the division, Czech lawyer Václav Mikulka, monitors the interpretation and occasionally completes it.
There's a working spirit and enthusiasm.
The seafood hides a previously little-known world, rich in natural resources.
Oil, ores and other mineral resources can be found here, but also hitherto unknown living organisms, which carry the key, for example, to the development of a cure for cancer.
A bottom larger than the mainland
Today, coastal states began vehemently applying for new undersea territories.
According to the U.N. convention, they use their rights, which were agreed in 1982 in Montego Bay in Jamaica.
It is mainly an area of the so-called continental shelf, which often extends up to several hundred kilometers from the coast of the country.
According to the Convention, it has an exclusive right to all resources of the continental shelf.
"Simplified, the continental shelf has that part of the seabed and its bedrock, which is a natural continuation of the continent under the sea.
It extends beyond the coastal sea, often beyond two hundred nautical miles of the so-called exclusive economic zone,” explains Mikulka.
The application submitted by that country to the Commission for the demarcation of the external borders of the continental shelf must be accompanied by extensive scientific and technological documentation based on many costly surveys and measurements.
It is the result of many years of preparatory work.
"One of our roles is to provide legal and professional technical assistance to the Commission.
So far, the Commission has discussed five applications, namely Russia, Brazil, Australia, Ireland and New Zealand.
Only three of the latter countries have succeeded so far," adds Mikulka.
The success of Australia, which was the first to substantially expand its undersea possessions, has recently received a global acclaim.
But he also acquired New Zealand - the area of the seabed, which the commission granted him last week, is about 1.6 million square kilometers, roughly six times larger than the size of the state itself.
Both countries have invested millions of dollars in the survey.
Profit, which will flow from the use of the seabed in the future, will, as they promise, be invaluable.
Other countries will gradually submit their applications.
They can do so within ten years of ratifying the "Montego Bay Convention."
Where the continental shelf ends, it begins with an area of the ocean floor, declared a "common heritage of humanity."
There is no state exclusive here anymore.
The right of mining in this area is granted by a convention-established international institution.
"The seabed is not a Klondajk," director Mikulka says in an exaggeration, adding that his "individual areas are legally clearly defined and their regime contractually enshrined."
Czechs in the Pacific
A part of the sea floor today also belongs to the Czech Republic.
They are shared with other states of the former Eastern bloc within the framework of the joint organization "Interoceanmetal".
According to the convention in 1991, like the investors of a few other states, it gained the status of a so-called "pioneering investor."
It is authorized to mine in the ClarionClipperton zone in the Pacific Ocean, located between Mexico, the Hawaian Islands and the Kiribati.
The investor-explored area is slightly larger than the former Czechoslovakia.
The bottom is rugged there, it is a kind of underwater replica of the Tatras.
"It can be mined on an area with a total area of 75 000 square kilometers.
The area is a rich site of polymetallic concretions containing nickel, copper, cobalt and manganese," states another Ind lawyer, Pakshi Rajan.
Rober Sandev, a Macedonian worker from the division, brings one such concrection to the demonstration.
It resembles a small potato or, with a little imagination, a fabulous shem who, according to legend, revived the Golem.
Germany's Special Deployment forces were hostaged during the release
The Egyptian government has appealed for help from Germany in resolving the hostage crisis - experts have arrived.
When the situation escalated, special units were prepared, but there was no deployment.
Further details of the liberation circumstances of eleven kidnapped European tourists in Egypt are gradually coming to the public.
According to data from the ministries of defence and the interior, German special forces were also indirectly involved in the rescue operation.
According to ministries, both experts of the Federal Army and members of the anti-terrorism group of the German police GSG9, squadrons of the Federal Police, the Federal Criminal Bureau, as well as logistics experts from the Technical Assistance Service (Technisches Hilfswerk) were at the site.
However, according to another announcement by the spokespersons of both ministries on Monday afternoon, there was no need for German forces, because the kidnappers let their captives go and fled when they recognized signs of violent liberation.
The Egyptian government is said to have accepted Germany's aid as support forces for on-the-spot action.
"Luckily, that didn't happen," said one spokesperson for the Federal Interior Ministry.
The attackers kidnapped a group of travelers consisting of five Germans, five Italians, one Romanian, as well as eight of their Egyptian guides on September 19 in the south of Egypt.
After kidnapping in a desert area in the south of Egypt, they moved hostages in border territories between Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad.
The identities of the kidnappers remained unclear until the last moment, as well as the details of the exemption or possible disbursement of the ransom.
They're already rescued safely in Cairo.
Minister Steinmeier said thostage liberation is in the care of the German embassy.
He did not comment on the details of his release.
There is not yet information available in Berlin when the rescue will return to Germany.
According to "Bild", all five tourists with special forces are expected to fly one of the German air forces and arrive in Germany on Tuesday.
The U.S. House of Representatives rejected the rescue package
The first chamber of the U.S
The Dow Jones index fell heavily.
At a ratio of 206 to 227 votes, the U.S. House of Representatives rejected the government's bailout package for financial markets.
In spite of all the challenges of U.S. President George W. Bush and the leading politicians of both parties, there was no majority for a program designed to alleviate chaos in the financial markets.
After all, proponents desperately tried to convince critics of a 700-billion-dollar package.
Barney Frank, one Democratic negotiator, urged MPs to give their consent before the vote, although, as he put it, the plan is not popular.
"It's hard to get political recognition for avoiding something that hasn't happened at all," he acknowledged.
The Dow Jones fell more than six percent, or 600 points, during the vote.
This was due to investor fears that the state bailout package could be forfeited.
Shortly before the end of the vote, the value of the index was raised, so that it fell sharply again after the announcement of the shipwrecked package.
Experts are now counting on other serious turbulences in international financial markets.
In order for the program to come into force, it is necessary to approve both the House of Representatives and the Senate.
Travel agents record increasing numbers of bookings
It looks like the winter will be good for German tourism entrepreneurs:
According to one study, travel bookings for the coming months increased significantly compared to the previous year - by more than 30 percent for the winter season.
Despite weak economic growth, the Germans' appetite to travel remains unvarnished.
For reservations for the winter season, travel agencies saw a 31 percent increase in turnover compared to the same period last year, as the German Travel Association (Deutscher Reiseverband (DRV) said on Monday in Berlin.
According to a survey by the GfK Market Research Institute, there are already increased numbers of bookings available for the autumn months.
According to DRV, the turnaround for October compared to 2007 was about eleven percent higher.
The basis for these results is the GfK numbers, which each month evaluates booking data from 1200 German travel agencies and interpolates them to the overall market.
This result included bookings of tours, which took place until the end of August 2008.
Due to the weak exchange rate of the dollar, trips to the U.S. are mainly in demand, DRV said.
For these trips, the increment of reservations is about one-third.
In addition, Spain, Turkey and Greece, as well as domestic waters, are particularly desirable targets for Germans.
In particular, organized tours are on course, DRV chairman Klaus Laepple said.
Considering the rising expenditure on energy and food, the cost here is "easier for the client than for an individually organized holiday".
Laepple explained that consumers can save money, among other things, by levying discounts for early booking or all-inclusive offers.
Even during the summer months, the number of bookings was very good, so compared to the previous year, there will again be a higher turnover - although according to another study, the number of Germans who undertook a trip declined compared to the previous year.
My First Oktoberfest
Endlessly repetitive pop, beer from blunters and prominent in costumes from well-known designers.
For the first time in a beer meadow.
Susanne Kronzucker and I don't really have much in common.
She's blonde, a TV presenter, and wears a tailor-made costume.
I've got dark hair, I'm a student, and my dress is from secondhand.
But one thing unites us: we're not even three minutes on the beer meadow yet, and we're already throwing photographers at us and nimbly full of memory cards - I'm flattered, after all, it's my first time at Oktoberfest.
That's how you can make friends.
Looks like my cheap costume has served its purpose.
I'm here as a tourist and I'm living north of the sausage equator, and so my sense of comfort in costume was rather limited.
But right after arriving at Theresienwiese, everything is better than it seemed.
Almost everyone is wearing a women's costume or leather pants; no one looks disgusted or embarrassingly touched - everything is completely normal.
Only my apron reaps the odd looks at the banana stand in chocolate.
One Municher warns me that I misuntied her: "You're vacant on the left, in the middle of a virgin, on the right, widowed in the back."
I'll secretly shift the bow from the back to the left.
"Okay," he says, laughing.
Others don't deal so much with subtle flirting techniques, and they're wearing simple felt hats with "To drunk to get dressed!" and sticking out naked buttocks at passers-by.
A young man with such exhibitionist tendencies is called Bob, he is from England and he came to Oktoberfest for the first time.
It looks "gorgeous" to him, with an extended "óóó".
That's what he tells me after he's relieved himself of just-drinken muza.
Right next to one of those hydrants that everyone thinks it's garbage bins.
Bob is, therefore, one of the dreaded "Wildbieslers," as is calleded freely urinating people in Bavaria.
Others are not lucky enough to get rid of alcohol in this way.
Around eight o'clock in the evening, they're jogging the area where Oktoberfest takes place, who have to take care of poor drinkers.
Paramedics always appear in swarms - in fours wearing a covered stretcher from tent to tent.
They work like most visitors to beer meadows: empty there, full back again.
Whoever lies under the sail of this coffin of piloage is not visible.
"So you can't go any further," one of the medics calls me in response to my question and disappears into the crowd.
Police have released the name of the victim of the accident
The name of a sixty-three-year-old woman who died in hospital after she was hit by a car in Essex.
An accident at Shenfield Road in Brentwood on Friday afternoon befalled Carole Paulson of Chelmsford Road.
She was taken to Queen's Hospital in Romford, but later died.
A fifty-one-year-old man was detained after the accident on suspicion of reckless driving.
He has already been discharged on police bail.
Turkey's diplomatic horizons are expanding
Long before Turkey began trying to join the European Union, European powers longed to dig deep into Turkey's borders.
On the eastern shore of the Bosphorus, perhaps only twenty paces in Asia stands one of the best monuments of this unsuccessful imperial ambition.
Hydarpasha railway station was designed by two German architects Otto Ritter and Helmut Con in the neo-Renaissance style.
That's what tourist guides will tell you.
The building sits astride at the end of the platforms with a tower on every corner of the frontage, indistinguishable from some provincial German town hall.
The stone's dirty.
The whole building is slowly disintegrating.
But once one gets inside the vaulted cash register, one can no longer have a doubt about the size of the business whose westernmost gate station was.
In August 1908, a German consortium of the Anatolian-Baghdad corporation opened the station.
Germany, which became involved in the game of empire late, intended to implement its ambitions to control the Middle East using this railway, which was supposed to lead to Damascus, Baghdad, and the territory that is now Saudi Arabia.
But Germany's imperial ambitions collapsed during the First World War.
Their tracks were attacked by Lawrence of Arabia and his irregular units.
Relative isolation from the surrounding territories, created by the frozen boundaries of the Cold War, is gone
British and colonial troops advanced from Egypt, pushing the Turks and their German allies out of Palestine and Syria.
And bad news came for Hydarpasha station, too.
In 1917, the explosion of an ammunition train destroyed its elaborate roof.
Hurkish sources say it was sabotage.
Since then it has gradually crumbled and history bypasses it.
Today, however, this gateway to Anatolia and further to the east serves - at least symbolically - to accentuate the expanding horizons of Turkish diplomacy.
The Ruling Justice and Development Party - the AKP - has created a completely new foreign policy for the country.
Relative isolation from the surrounding territories, created by the frozen borders of the Cold War, is gone.
Now a new policy is being applied - Turkey is engaged.
Remarkably, the Turkish government has good relations with Israel and Syria, with Palestinian factions Hamas and Fatah, with Iraq and Iran and, of course, with the European Union and the United States.
In order to examine the parameters of this new policy, I boarded a night train to the Turkish capital Ankara, where I was supposed to meet with one of AKP's leading experts on foreign policy, the Turkish MP Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning - about ten and a half hours later - after a creaky ride on the tracks that commemorated the old-world rhythms of the well-known documentary Night Post, I arrived in the Turkish metropolis, designed as a new city for a secular state that wants to turn its back on its Ottoman past.
However, with its well-known Islamist roots, the AKP represents a shift in the paradigms of the Turkish consensus.
In the west, it raised suspicions, especially in Washington.
The taxi brought me to AKP's main tent, an ultra-modern brick-and-mortar building, deliberately built in an unfolding suburb, surrounded by half-built department stores and power handlers, where medium-sized executives in commercial suits - both men and women - meet to make early morning shops.
Suat Kiniklioglu could easily be a centre-right politician from almost every other European political party.
Jacket, tie, I'm sure Blackberry did, too.
"The aim of the party's foreign policy is to reintegrate Turkey into its immediate surroundings.
The Cold War was an anomaly," he told me.
Turkey has been cut off its border.
Today he has to look to the east and the west at the same time
It wasn't a choice.
It had to do both.
"But there's definitely going to come," I asked, 'when Turkey might have to make a decision?"
"Turkey," he said, "didn't want such a decision to happen."
Then he emphasized what almost everyone I was talking to here was almost memorized: Turkish relations not only with the Middle East, but also with the Caucasus and around the Black Sea are just as good for Europe and the United States as Turkey.
With Turkey on board, it appears that Europe as a whole can, metaphorically speaking, drive on these tracks to the east to Asia and the Middle East.
Back at Hydarpasha station, after another mostly sleepy night, I was thinking of those two German gentlemen Herr Rittera and Herr Con, who were watching this building build a hundred years ago.
How surprised they would if they found that European ambitions in the Middle East could now be, for the most part, in Turkish hands.
And when I was waiting in the harbour for the ferry back through the Bosphorus, I remembered that in those days Turkey was called "sick man of Europe".
So it seems that at least then there was not much doubt which continent should be entitled to this fascinating country.
Memorable Lives: Vernon Handley and William Woodruff
Mark Brown writes: As Guildford's music leader since 1962, Vernon "Tod" Handley (obituary, 9/11) was in charge of the Guildford Philharmonic orchestra and choir.
As part of the county's musical plan, Proteus founded an excellent choir of young Proteus for a year
He was supposed to be until he was 21, but by the time Proteus sang for the last time in 1979, several of us were almost 30 - we simply refused to give up the choir.
"Proteus" (mythological shape changer) represented the willingness and ability of the choir to embark on any music that was presented to it - whether madrigals or Messiaen, either independently or with the entire orchestra.
The musical adaptability created by such a young choir was often stunning, always exciting and all thanks to the inspiration of that one man and the enthusiasm for music that remained completely unchangeable all the time.
How often only Tod told us to "leave the music to speak for itself."
He said: "The composer knows what he wants.
It's all in the music.
Then sing it! '
His baton technique was absolutely clear and precise, but it was neither dictatorial nor flowery.
Wherever there was a musical standard, he had always lifted it.
John Frayn Turner writes:
If any conductor was to be knighted, then it was Vernon Handley.
I knew him well in the two decades of conducting the Guildford Philharmonic from 1962 to 1982.
I've reviewed more than 100 of his concerts over the past about 40 years and I can honestly say I've never been disappointed with any of them - and I've been inspired many times.
Tod Handley had such a great combination of size and personal modesty.
He hated conductors who, according to him, simply "pulled one or two soaked rocks around the world."
Many of us will miss it.
William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
Your obituary William Woodruff (25 September) claims that "in 1946 he returned to economic history at Oxford and in 1950 he became a researcher at Houblon-Norman... while also studying at BSc, MA and PhD."
This is based on Woodruff's note by the author in Beyond Nab End, but it's not accurate.
From 1946 to 1953 he taught economic history at the University of Nottingham, and at that time he completed a dissertation on the British rubber industry, which was led by Professor J. D. Chambers, and subsequently published it in 1958 as a publication by the British rubber industry.
Indeed, he spented 1950-51 in America as a researcher at Houblon-Norman and the summer of 1952 at the University of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953, he left Nottingham and moved to Illinois.
It's not known why Woodruff went over that part of his career.
J. Vicente Resino at the Faculty of Arts
"I'm recommending José Vicente Resina's "Flamenco and Life" exhibition because his documentary black-and-white photographs are amazing.
I was impressed by the simplicity and powerful charge with which it affects the viewers, with thoughtfulness and respect for photographers.
An exhibition that shows a lot of feeling.
Resino can capture magical moments.”
Honda attacks for the second time with its bestseller
In the month of November, Honda will begin the sale of the second-generation
If the predecessor of this single-spatial vehicle already has considerable space, it now offers an even more spacious interior.
This is accompanied by improved engines, which increase driving comfort and allow for greater fuel efficiency.
Evolution rather than revolution.
Such is the second generation of Honda Jazz.
Although, from an aesthetic point of view, the lines of the new Jazz are very similar to the previous model, there have been many changes on this single-space car.
It is worth mentioning, for example, the innovative design, a new platform, larger dimensions and new engines.
It should be noted even before we explore the new model that the new Jazz is coming to market six years after the launch of the first generation.
That’s why the first thing we’ll notice is the fact that Honda needed a shorter time to rejuvenate this model than other manufacturers.
Although Jazz is a lesser-known model in Europe, it has become a phenomenon in sales in other markets.
This is the case of Japan, the country where Jazz was the best-selling car in 2002, as well as during 2008.
It has sold almost a million units in Japan since the launch of Honda Jazz.
The second market became an old continent with about half a million units sold in six years.
Honda, however, aims to boost new Jazz sales in its category.
Thus, while in 2007 this Japanese company sold a total of 83 000 pieces of Honda Jazz, in 2009 estimates accounted for 100 000 cars, which would represent an increase of 17% on the European market.
When it comes to the Spanish market, Honda has sold a total of 8,000 Jazz since 2002, which translates to 6.7% of Honda's total sales in Spain.
When it comes to a new generation of Jazz, the management of the Spanish branch expects to sell 2,000 units for the whole year.
Just the arrival of the new Jazz will allow Honda to significantly mitigate the decline in sales in Spain.
In fact, the automaker's management expects the end of the year with a total of 22,000 units sold, which translates to 4,000 fewer than in 2007 and a decrease of 16%.
Despite this increase in sales, Honda increased its share of the Spanish market to 1.8%, compared to 1.6% last year.
In 2010, the Japanese company expects it to reach 2%.
In order to achieve this share, Honda relies on the success of its next new model.
It is Honda Insight, a five-door hybrid of a compact size with a length of 4.37 meters, from which the Japanese company promises worldwide sales of approximately 200 000 units a year.
In terms of markets, according to the Japanese manufacturer's estimates, 50 per cent of Insight will be sold in the United States, while the remaining percentages will be divided by the old continent and Japan.
Honda Insight is expected to launch in the first months of next year as soon as it is unveiled to the public in Valencia in January.
Compared to the first generation, all dimensions of the new Jazz have increased.
Its length increased up to 3.9 meters (five centimeters more), the width to 1.69 meters (two centimeters more) and the wheelbase to 2.5 meters, which means an extension of five centimeters.
On the contrary, the height has not changed and is still 1.52 meters.
The increase in dimensions has also increased the interior space.
This advantage was achieved both by using a new platform and storing the fuel tank in the middle of the car, which was already used in the previous Honda Jazz.
Also, the volume of the luggage compartment - which has a double bottom system - increased up to 399 liters, 19 liters more than the volume of the luggage compartment in the first generation.
It should be noted that these dimensions occur with the 1.4 engine version, as the luggage capacity of the Honda Jazz 1.2 is 379 liters.
In addition to the exterior elements - such as rear LED lights or the possibility of equipping with a glass panoramic roof - makes it easier to access the rear seats.
Doors leading to the second row of seats allow an 80-degree opening angle compared to 67 for the first Jazz model.
In the second generation of the Honda Jazz there are also new engines.
Initially, this small model will be offered with two gasoline engines 1.2 and 1.4 liters.
Both engines use i-VTEC technology, which allows for lower consumption, lower carbon dioxide emissions and delivers excellent improvements to the propulsion system at the same time with high torque in its entirety of engine speed.
The first 1.2-litre engine has an output of 90 hp at 6,000 rpm with a maximum torque of 114 Nm at 4,900 rpm.
This engine - which had 78 hp in the previous generation of the Jazz car - has a consumption of 5.3 liters and an emission of 125 grams of CO2.
Compared to the previous engine, which in the previous generation reached 83 hp, it will currently reach 100.
The 100-horsepower Honda Jazz 1.4 has a combined consumption of 5.4 liters and an emission of 128 grams of CO2.
"Car bee" prevents precipitation
This time, Nissan was inspired to develop accident-protecting technologies in nature, specifically in bees.
This Japanese company has created a robot that takes the pattern of flying bees and thus prevents collisions.
Such is Bio-mimetic Car Robot Drive or BR23C, which represents the first step in precipitation prevention technologies.
In collaboration with the Centre for Scientific and Technological Research at the University of Tokyo, the engineers of the Nissan Motor Co. robotics Centre for Technological Advances have developed the prestigious University of Tokyo.
According to Kazuhir Doi, director-general of public relations for technology, research and brand development, the BR23C is presented as an extension of Nissan's four safety shield zones.
The robot is tasked with strengthening the ability to prevent collisions in cars of this brand.
During the flight, each bee creates its own oval-shaped personal space, very similar to Nissan's safety shield.
The most important, however, are compound bee eyes, which are able to see in all directions at an angle of more than 300 degrees, allowing the bee to fly in its personal space without stopping.
In creating this visual function, the engineers suggested what they called the Laser Range Finder.
It detects obstacles that are located up to two meters within a 180-degree radius before BR23C, calculates the distance of the object and sends a signal to the on-board microprocessor, which immediately converts it to a collision warning.
"At the moment an obstacle is recorded, the robot mimics the bee's movements and immediately changes direction by turning the wheels to a right or even greater angle to prevent a collision.
The biggest difference compared to any existing system is the fact that all evasive manoeuvres are absolutely instinctive.
If this weren't, the robot wouldn't be able to react fast enough to avoid obstacles," explains Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the Mobile Laboratory and chief engineer of the project.
Unlike bees, the robot cannot move up and down or diagonally, its movement is limited to only two dimensions and the direction in which it is allowed to rotate its wheels, but not in an infinite number of directions and possibilities as with bees.
In addition to acceleration and deceleration, Nissan also used the rotation function to prevent the robot from colliding.
"The device only needs to process the information every few seconds and, according to this data.
It does not require storage of information or logical intuition from previous manoeuvres, thanks to which it can operate without interruption with only two dozen kB of memory.
It may surprise you, but we're not talking about a gig or a mega."
This is the first step in the development of new instantaneous collision prevention technologies, a technology in which Nissan predicts use in serial cars in a decade.
The robot uses lithium polymer batteries, which are lighter and last longer than lithium-ion.
Another possible future use of this technology could be the incorporation of a unit into the GPS satellite navigation system, which would lead the driver to their destination while avoiding obstacles on the way.
Enjoy your fears before taking out a mortgage
It must be acknowledged that the U.S. Treasury Department, apart from having enough capital to hand out injections, also lacks ingenuity.
In the face of the crisis, and to prevent Americans from taking high-risk mortgages at banks with dubious solvency, it has created a popular-educational "Bad Credit Hotel" game online.
It's "Hotel of Bad Loans"... or a confused hotel.
The hotel, and especially its receptionists, look grim to think twice, twice, whether it’s the right time to apply for a loan.
And, above all, where to apply, which is probably her intention.
The fact is that the game contains useful information, although not very detailed.
It's a site suitable for the youth rather than space for the entire public.
And what happens when the game is over?
A room of dreams will appear inside the hotel.
At least something, because you better not talk about buying a house...
Morgan Stanley is opening up its MUFG capital
Morgan Stalen has announced that Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group (MUFG), Japan’s largest bank, is entering the center, issuing nine billion dollars to raise 21% of its capital. The receipt of this report was accompanied by a significant decline in its exchange rate.
A week after revealing their preliminary deal, the two banks explained that MUFG intends to buy 9.9 percent of Morgan Stanley's current shares at a uniform price of $25.25, representing a total of $3 billion.
The agreed price is 19 % lower than the book value of the securities as at 31 August.
MUFG is investing an additional six billion dollars into acquiring convertible securities that will provide it with a 10% return, with an exchange price of US$31.25.
Morgan Stanley specified that the entire operation will take place on the basis of an averaging uniform price of 29 dollars, i.e. 28% below its current exchange rate.
Still, Morgan Stanley shares lost 6.79%, rising to 23.07 dollars around 17,00 Greenwich time.
"This strategic merger represents an important opportunity on the way to accelerating Morgan Stanley's transition to banking holding status," explained John Mack, chief executive officer of this U.S. group.
The new statute was adopted by Morgan Stanley last week, which is supposed to allow him to diversify his activities, but at the same time puts it under the supervision of the Federal Reserve Bank.
Its merger with MUFG also confirms that the current financial crisis is forcing Wall Street's biggest aces to take a far more cautious approach than before.
Among other things, the conditions presented on Monday show that the deal has been changed over the past few days: last Monday Morgan only announced that it would sell up to 20 percent of its shares to the Japanese for about $8.5 billion.
"Just a week ago, we thought," Toshihide Mizuno, MUFG's chief executive, explained in Tokyo, "that our participation will consist entirely of ordinary shares.
However, in view of the current situation of economics and the market, we wanted to limit the risks to a minimum and also cover ourselves.”
Based on the weighed average price, MUFG will pay 7% less for Morgan Stanley securities than their book value.
And the operation will result in a reduction in earnings from the stock by about 20%.
The agreement foresees that MUFG will be able to convert half of its preferential securities into ordinary shares in a year if the latter will be exchanged for a certain period of time at a price 50 % in excess of the exchange price, i.e. at around 47 dollars.
Morgan Stanley assures that the capital raised by MUFG will allow him to take advantage of investment opportunities, strengthen his balance sheet and increase his cash.
However, a spokeswoman for the group declined to provide specific information about the strategic projects of the two new allies.
After MUFG’s investment, Morgan Stanley should boast of the Tier One’s solvency ratio of around 15%, one of the best in the industry.
Morgan specified that he had "significantly" lowered his balance sheet since the end of August.
Recent weeks have been challenging for the group, as its exchange rate has fallen by half, while insurance costs have risen sharply against the risk of non-payment associated with debt.
These costs picked up the opposite development on Monday, but remained high, around 12%.
Last week Goldman Sachs, the only other "survivors" from Wall Street's big investment banks after Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers collapsed and Merrill Lynch buyout that it collected €15bn, partly from Berkshire Hathaway, the Warren Buffett group.
Rafael Correa in Quito on Monday.
To re-establish a republic around the socialism of the 20th century.
Such is the joint project of Venezuelan Hugo Chavez, Bolivian Eva Morales and Ecuadorian Rafael Correa.
Last Sunday, the third-appointed person submitted to the vote, following the example of his counterparts and friends, a proposal for a new Constitution.
And his mission is accomplished because 64% of voters, according to official results covering 80% of the vote, voted "yes" in the referendum.
"No" according to these results won 28%, 7 percent of the votes were invalid and 0.7% of ballots were empty.
In order for the draft Constitution to be adopted, it had to receive half of the vote plus one.
Two polls carried out at the exit of polling stations and the NGO's calculation predicted a clear "yes" victory on Sunday, situating them within 63% to 70%.
Rafael Correa, after all, demanded the victory of his draft Constitution on the night from Sunday to Monday.
"The Constitution won all along the line," he assured, congratulating the Ecuadorians at Guayaquil governorship's residence, 280 kilometers southwest of Quito.
It goes without saying that Rafael Correa received congratulations from Chávez and Morales, but also from his Chilean counterpart, Michelle Bachelet.
According to the observation mission of the Organization of States of America (OEA), the vote concerned a text of 444 points building "new socialism" promoted by the president.
The proposal of the Constitution sanctifies the power of the state over economics by reserving it to "plan the development" of a country living primarily from the extraction of diesel by foreign companies and from the foreign exchange sent by its emigrants.
The proposal, backed by Correa, which was elected in 2006, also extends the powers of the head of state, who will be able to carry out two consecutive four-year mandates and will have the opportunity to dissembate the House.
Correa has already announced that he will use this power and apply for a new mandate in February 2009.
Among other things, the constitution strengthens the principle of free health and education, which the president has started to implement in a country with 13.9 million inhabitants, where 50 percent of the population lives below the poverty line.
Finally, it also envisages a ban on the presence of foreign troops, thus ending an agreement between Washington and Quito on the use of the Ecuadorian base as the starting point of U.S. drug operations.
19 hostages have been liberated
The Egyptian commandos took eleven tourists and their eight-member escort, who were captured ten days ago.
They're in Cairo.
Eleven European tourists and their eight Egyptian guides, who have been held captivated in the desert for ten days, were alive and healthily transported to the Egyptian capital on Monday.
During the morning, Egyptian commandos liberated them in Chad.
Hostages arrived around 15.30, apparently in good form, aboard a special plane to the al-Maza military base, closely adjacent to Cairo's international airport.
Immediately they were transported, without having the opportunity to express themselves in the press, aboard a helicopter to a hospital in Maadi, the outskirts of a residential area of Cairo to be subjected to medical examinations there.
"They are completely healthy" and "no ransom has been paid to the hijackers," Egyptian tourism minister Zoheir Garrana said on the airport's taxiway.
Since its captivity on September 19 during a four-wheel-drive safari vehicle at the forefront of Mount Uanat, on the borders of Egypt, Sudan and Libya, a group of hostages have been dragged from one stash to another by 35 unidentified hijackers.
The group includes 11 tourists - five Italians, five Germans and one Romanian - as well as eight Egyptians: two guides, four drivers, one Aegyptous's border-in-chief, safari organizer.
Some leader of the Egyptian security forces hinted that 30 members of the special forces, who approached the site aboard two helicopters, set off for a rundown during the night just before dawn.
"There was a gunfight, half of the hijackers were killed and the others escaped," said this source, who wished to remain anonymous.
He indicated that a total of 150 members of the Egyptian Special Forces from the "lightning" unit were sent to Sudan, where they received the support of German and Italian commandos.
The head of Italian diplomacy Franco Frattini from Belgrade, where he is currently located, mentioned the role played by secret agents and components of Italian special forces.
"We have to recognize that we have achieved this result thanks to the professionalism and efficiency of the people of our secret services, from special forces," he said.
He also denied a ransom has been paid.
On the eve, the Sudanese army said it had detained a four-wheel-driven vehicle on board, killing six, including a Chadian commander and arresting two.
The army claimed that they were linked to a group of rebels in Darfur, the Movement for the Liberation of Sudan - Unity (SLA-U), which the movement immediately rejected.
Letters - The silent majority of the culture department
The debate about the financing of culture fills the pages of the newspaper with inch-long headlines and is the theme of the current election campaign.
All the better.
However, one area of our cultural life is still neglected.
Who's going to rise to denounce the poor sums that are provided to small museums, archives, archaeological excavations, and national memorable places that are starving?
Which politician dares to remind the federal government that he is omitting his monuments and his historic buildings?
That in Québek, as in Canada, we don't have laws that really protect our cultural heritage?
Did you know that the French government devotes three and a half million euros to the restoration of just Chinon Castle in the Loire Valley?
With our U.S. Neighbors, it is written in the Constitution that the protection of cultural heritage is part of the culture of Americans, and that this heritage is to be protected by all citizens, by all elected representatives and by all regional organizations, in synergy.
In Québek, in the Federation of Companies for the History of Québek, at least 40 000 volunteers brought together into 211 societies for history, monuments, archives and genealogy strive to uncover history, preserve piles of private archives, and protect archaeological remnants for a few.
It is often precisely these companies who are the watchdogs of our monuments, driven by hopeless protest when promoters and leaders release their bulldozers to old stones and forgotten cemeteries.
When will we finally see a grandstand for history and monuments?
Benelux government national part of Fortis bank
Benelux states will help the Belgian-Dutch bank Fortis, which was the first in Europe to get into trouble due to the spreading effect of the U.S. Mortgage Market crisis, with partial nationalization: the trio of countries will support a monetary and insurance institution, which also has capital participation in Hungary, amounting to 11.2 billion euros.
It was agreed this weekend at the highest level by the Belgian, Luxemburg and Dutch governments, with the participation of the President of the European Central Bank and the European Commissioner for Competition.
Fortis is the first in the eurozone to be considered as a precedent as precedent in the context of the international financial market crisis, which has been spreading in waves for more than a year.
In the sense of the decision, which was born on Sunday night, all three states will buy out almost half (49 percent) of the bank’s assets.
The bank will with absolute certainty sell part of its capital participations, especially - mainly insurance - activities purchased last year from the Dutch bank ABN Amro.
Fortis shares began to fall sharply last week, in the second half of the week there were already serious signs of a crisis at the institute.
However, analysts agree that, with its 85,000 employees, the bank is too big to let it go bankrupt.
Securities of the firm fell just 12 percent on Friday, to a 14-year low.
Since January, the value of shares has fallen by more than two-thirds.
Due to current share prices, the group's total market capital value is around 14 billion euros, but this is unrealistic, according to business leaders.
In fact, the company’s management accuses uncertainty about the fall in Fortis shares in the overseas market related to the adoption of a £70 billion U.S. package plan.
By Monday, the stock had already risen slightly.
On Friday, new CEO Filip Dierckx was appointed to head the bank.
The money institution was also looking for private buyers, but with a more serious offer, no one has yet come forward.
The Belgian government has previously announced that investors do not have to worry, they will not lose their money.
There has been no panic among the bank's clients so far, and branches have not been taken by attack.
In Hungary, Fortis has a separate branch and has also purchased several leasing companies.
A fox in a chicken coop
Life and health insurers in America, based on people's data on drug purchases, often reject the possibility of concluding an insurance contract with a bidder.
Data protection experts fear the data market will become uncontrollable.
"There is a data protection program that binds the use of drug prescribing data in any case to the consent of the person concerned.
It has to be in the hands of someone independent, otherwise we entrust a fox cooper," Richard Dick, the author of a drug prescribing program, was quoted as saying.
Insurance companies make premiums and amounts paid in the case of claims dependent on the health status of candidates, and if the risk is assessed as too large, they will not even take out insurance with it.
Previously, they sent them for medical examinations and concluded the contract only when they knew their results.
Ten years ago Dick devised a much cheaper procedure: based on an identification number, the pharmacies customer can easily find out what medication he bought in the last year and a half, from which the program itself will deduce what disease he suffers from and what his medical condition is.
For example, if he regularly buys a large number of cholesterol-reducing drugs, it refers to the fact that he has virtually uncontrollably high levels of blood fats, which immediately puts him in a category with a higher risk.
There are several versions of the program in circulation, some determine the risk coefficient at the same time, so the insurance company has almost no work left, although minor difficulties arise.
One of the disadvantages is that many drugs can also be used for several purposes, for example, some compounds are usable against migraines or hot flashes, but they are also effective against depression.
From the point of view of commercial insurance companies, these represent an important risk-increasing factor, whereas the first mentioned difficulties are not.
In order to request and process data, special companies have been created, which, before obtaining the necessary information from pharmacies, will ask the patient for permission.
They are now forced to do so by the authorities, even though they have not punished them for their negligence so far.
Some companies have recently been processing the results of laboratory examinations, which they associate with data about medications used, thanks to which they can provide an even more accurate picture of the health status of patients.
More and more companies have access to the data, at the same time monitoring their use will become increasingly difficult.
There is a special law on the protection of health data, but its effectiveness applies only to health service providers and insurance companies.
The bill, which gives authorities the power to control even businesses specializing in the provision of data, is before Congress.
Richard Dick acknowledges that this is not enough: he has therefore developed his programme in such a way that the assignment of the data is tied to the explicit consent of the person concerned.
The other thing is that he can hardly deny the consent in question, otherwise the insurance companies will not conclude a contract with him.
So after all, chickens are somewhat dependent on fox's goodwill.
The Global Marseille Menu
The European Union and India will double the turnover of trade
Since the European Union and India are mutual leading trading partners, this topic has been dominated, in addition to other, largely global problems, also on the agenda of the ninth meeting at the highest level.
There will also be a Franco-Indian summit today, which will focus primarily on atomic energy cooperation.
Indeed, the European Union recognises that India is an emerging democratic power that should be given a greater opportunity to speak into international affairs, but member countries have not yet managed to find a common denominator on supporting New Delhi's permanent membership in the UN Security Council.
This issue has not even been further discussed at yesterday's ninth summit in Marseille.
French President Nicolas Sarkozy as the current president of the Union, or the president of the European Commission, José Manuel Barroso, and senior foreign policy official Javier Solana welcomed Indian Prime Minister Manmohan Singh, with whom they have also agreed that the turnover of trade will double within five years, or rise to more than one hundred billion euros.
Given that between 2000 and 2007 the exchange of goods has risen from 25 to 55 billion, this is not an overly ambitious target.
Twenty-six existing bilateral agreements with India on civil aviation have been aligned from a legal point of view, so air travel has also been given the green light in the future.
By the end of 2009, India would like to sign a free trade agreement with the Union.
Sarkozy has vowed that negotiations will be accelerated.
The aim is to offset the balance sheet, while the EU is India's first trading partner, New Delhi is ranked ninth among EU partners.
The summit also paid attention to global affairs.
The situation in Afghanistan and Pakistan, or terrorism - both sides in all its forms have condemned it and called for an expeditious convening of the UN conference.
They have also urged an end to the Doha trade negotiations round, although they both have a large share of his previous setback.
The European Union has confirmed that it will insist on its energy and climate programme that it would like to reduce emissions of harmful substances by at least 20 percent by 2020.
India has also agreed with this, as well as the need to conclude a global agreement at next year's climate conference, reducing the greenhouse-effected gas production by 50 percent by 2050.
Both parties would also like to prevent food prices from rising and supporting the convening of an international conference to analyse the financial crisis.
India's premiership arrives today in the French capital, where, in a bilateral meeting, it is preparing to make a deal regarding the peaceful use of atomic energy.
Paris believes that French companies will receive mandates for the construction of Indian nuclear reactors worth twenty billion euros.
According to agency reports, the agreement would be valid for 15 years.
More than three hundred Italian publishers in Frankfurt
The great participation of Italian publishers at the international book fair in Frankfurt.
More than three hundred Italian publishers will take part in Buchmesse's 60th edition, the most important international meeting for the exchange of rights.
At this edition, the International Frankfurt Book Fair awaits more than seven thousand exhibitors from one hundred different countries, who will occupy 171,790-square-square-meter fair with more than four hundred and three thousand titles to see, with Turkey as a guest of honor.
Deputy Minister of Culture Francesco Maria Giro will represent the Italian government at the inaugural festivities in the Italian Pavilion: Italy will in fact participate institutionally with the Punto Italia – on a 264-square-foot exhibition area.
This will be followed by a meeting with the press in the Buchmesse Room Structure in Hall 5.0: it will be an opportunity to present numbers, tendencies and perspectives in terms of the state of publishing in Italy.
According to the data, the importance of books by foreign authors in relation to the total Italian production continues to decrease, mainly in relation to literature for children and adults.
Steve Fossett's plane, a millionaire who disappeared a year ago
The rest of Steve Fossett's plane, a millionaire who disappeared on September 3, 2007, was found while flying the desert in Nevada.
The debris was discovered on one plateau at an altitude of three thousand meters, in the Mammoth Lakes region of Sierra Nevada, on the border between California and Nevada.
There were no human remains inside the aircraft.
Madera County sheriff, John Anderson, dispensed doubts that still remained: "The plane was found, we sent the team there, and now we're sure it's exactly what Steve Fossett flew in on the day he disappeared," he said at a press conference.
Thus, the sheriff specified that human remains had not been found either on the plane or in the surrounding area.
The 63-year-old millionaire has been presumed dead since February.
The U.S. Transportation Safety Bureau (Ntsb) announced that a few hours after the debris was recognized, an investigative team was dispatched that eventually identified the plane: this is exactly the Bellanca 8kcab, missing since September 3, 2007, when Fossett took off from Yerington, Nevada, for a three-hour flight.
Yesterday, still in California, not far from the border with Nevada, some of Fossett's personal items were found by one pair near their dwelling in Mammoth Lakes.
These are two documents with Fossett's name, both issued by the Illinois Federal Aviation Administration, about money in cash and one jacket.
Fossetto's search has been interrupted a month after his disappearance.
The billionaire won a place in history thanks to a long collection of primacies.
In 2002, on his sixth attempt, he became the first man to complete a round-the-climmage trip in an air balloon: two week flight alone.
Three years later he circled the world on a plane and without landing.
His many records also include a flight at an altitude of 15.445 meters, above the Andes, albeit with a copilot.
Glk, an ad you don't expect
One unusual Suv with an original design and precisely defined decisive lines, wide surfaces with precise corners.
This is, in short, Glk's last birth from "compact" jeeps of the star's house, by which Mercedes-Benz tried to bring a whirlwind of novelties into this sector.
Thanks to its unconventional processing, there will be an unexpected and unconventional communication that will accompany the launch of Suv Glk and which will be available in Mercedes-Benz showrooms from next 17 October.
In this way, an advertising campaign accompanying the debut of a new vehicle will start an unofficial mode of communication also thanks to a futuristic poster campaign in 3D, which is thus used in Italy for the first time for a car brand.
The maxi poster project, divided into three different phases, will be dominated by Milan's main business class, Corso Como, until November 15.
The first phase of this new type of communication will begin with the introduction of the car in stereoscopic vision, and with the help of special glasses that will be handed out near the Corsa Como, it will be possible to see the new SUV in almost actual dimensions and in a three-dimensional format.
Truly, if you look at the picture, you will have the impression that the car will come out of the poster.
After that, thanks to the use of innovative Elumina8 technology, the new Glk will be enlightened at different moments in all its trifles, so that it can then be reassembled into a single image.
Without a doubt, Mercedes is trying to introduce something new into the stereotypical area of car communication.
Will he be able to arouse the interest of future customers in this way? We’ll see.
Now we know who Italian hooligans are
From Triveneto, they're risking Daspa.
Who are those 150-200 boys and girls for whom Italy is now ashamed? They are part of the group "Ultras Italia", which has been following Nazionale azzura for years, and which has so far, in fact, never caused exaggerated problems.
She has never even met fans of her opponent.
The orientation of the group is right-wing.
The founders come from Trivenet.
From Verona, Padua, Trieste e Udine.
In Sophia they were joined by groups (in fact few groups) from Campania and Puglia.
They have three-colored flags, on which the cities of the origin of the participants appear: on Saturday night, they removed them so that they would not be recognized.
They are inspired by English fans.
In Bulgaria they found true Nazi fans, Levsky.
Figc gave Italian fans 144 tickets for the match: everyone in the name, i.e. easily controllable.
And Viminale actually checked them all: among those who went to Sophia there was no one with the ongoing Dasle, no one who was forbidden to visit stadiums.
The four blue fans, who had earlier offences from the stadium, were denied a ticket and the match was banned.
Now Viminale is waiting to read the messages coming from the Bulgarian police, then make arrangements: In this case it is possible to give Daspo for five years at least to fans who have been arrested or to those who have been identified during the riot.
International Daspo, which forbids visiting not only Italian stadiums, but also throughout Europe.
Our hooligans, in short, won't set foot in a single stadium anymore.
To happiness.
But now there is a fear of emulation: that is, more than a hundred and fifty will be added to those.
"We have to put out this fire right away and not justify those who behave in this way," Viminal said.
So, "properly open eyes, mainly because of the national match on Wednesday in Leč".
There's a small core of supporters in Puglia right now.
Osservatorio will meet tomorrow, on Tuesday Casms, which can also count on one part of Aise, it is a secret service that cares about problems in stadiums.
"Choral Führer-Leader and stretched arms during Mameli's anthem? It's not a crime in Bulgaria...".
Such is the idea of Domenico Mazzilli (expressed in a really very happy way), who has been the director of the Osservatorium of Viminals for a few weeks (he won a place that was formerly Francesco Tagliente and then Felice Ferlizzi).
Mazzilli was formerly a bursar in Trieste, where the core of Ultras Italia's supporters, founded in 2002, is located.
It's true, as Mazzilli says, that it's not a crime in Bulgaria to glorify leaders, but of course the behaviour of these 150-200 fans is an embarrassment.
"I'm not a sociologist - Osservatora's number one adds-crimes are attributed to the country in which they happened.
So far, this group has remained within limits, now let's take a hard look at what happened and evaluate for the future.
But let’s also talk about the whistling at Mameli’s anthem: it’s not even a crime, but if you don’t mind, it’s rudeness.”
